        "findings": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change slots cleanly into the established OfficeFloor idioms of this codebase, with no restructuring warranted:\n\n- **`RequirePostcodeForCity`** mirrors the existing `Require*` validation family (`RequireCityCapacity`, `RequireOwnerFields`, `RequireUniqueIdentity`): same `service(@Val OwnerFieldsDto owner)` signature, same early-return-when-absent guard style, same \"throws a checked escalation\" contract. The optional/when-present logic lives in exactly the right unit \u2014 a dedicated validation step \u2014 rather than being smuggled into `ApplyOwner` or `ValidateOwner`.\n- **`PostcodeOutOfRangeException`** matches `CityAtCapacityException` (checked, carries the offending values, javadoc naming its thrower and handler). Storing `postcode`/`city` even though the handler emits only an `errors` array is consistent with `CityAtCapacityException`, not an inconsistency.\n- **`PostcodeOutOfRangeExceptionHandler`** faithfully mirrors `InvalidTelephoneExceptionHandler`'s `errors`-array shape and 400 response.\n- **`Localities.postcodeRange`** is the natural home for the region\u2192range table \u2014 it lives beside the city\u2192region table it depends on, and reuses `of(city)` rather than duplicating the mapping. No catch-all growth; the class stays a focused lookup helper.\n- YAML wiring (escalation file, step insertion with `govern: [ transaction ]`, `next:` chaining) and the parallel model/schema/openapi/`ApplyOwner` edits all follow the existing conventions.\n\nLogic is in the right unit, nothing is duplicated, and a maintainer of this codebase would find the additions unsurprising."
